# Domain and Application Layer

Universal rule. Transport and framework specifics live in `ruby-conventions.mdc`, `vue-frontend.mdc`, and `design-principles.mdc`.

Chatwoot uses pragmatic Rails layering — not a pure DDD folder split. Map concepts like this:

| Layer | Chatwoot location | Responsibility |
|-------|-------------------|------------------|
| **Domain** | `app/models`, `lib/` | Business language, invariants, validations, identity |
| **Application** | `app/services`, `app/finders`, `app/builders` | One business action per class; orchestration across models/ports |
| **Transport** | `app/controllers`, `app/jobs`, Vue components | HTTP/API/UI wiring — delegate, don't implement rules |
| **Infrastructure** | `app/services/**/providers`, adapters, integrations | External APIs, channel providers, persistence details |

## Domain (`app/models`, `lib/`)

- Models express business invariants via validations, enums, scopes, and focused instance methods
- Keep models free of HTTP params, controller context, and channel-specific API calls
- Prefer rich model behavior when the rule truly belongs to the entity; move cross-entity workflows to services
- Value-like objects (immutable, compared by value) can live in `lib/` when they don't need persistence

## Application (`app/services`, `app/finders`)

- Each service represents **one business action** with a small public API (`#perform`, `#call`, or `#execute`)
- Controllers and jobs call services — they do not contain business rules
- Finders/builders handle query composition and object assembly, not HTTP response shaping
- Do not access `ENV`, raw HTTP types, or socket payloads inside services — pass explicit inputs
- Return explicit outcomes: raise custom exceptions from `lib/custom_exceptions/` or return structured data — avoid bare booleans or magic strings

## Transport (controllers, Vue)

- Controllers: strong params → authorize → call service → render JSON
- Vue components: presentation only; business logic in composables or store actions
- Map domain/application failures to HTTP status or UI messages at the transport layer

## Boundaries

- Application logic never depends on concrete provider internals — depend on base classes or injected adapters (see `Whatsapp::Providers::BaseService`)
- New use cases go in `app/services/`, not controllers or models
- Fork-specific business logic: prefer `custom/` overlay; mark upstream edits with `# FORK:` (see `fork-strategy.mdc`, `fork-merge-conflicts.mdc`)
